{"content":"The regression was in sqlparse/engine/grouping.py's group_where function. The WHERE clause grouping used `tlist.tokens[eidx - 2]` to find the end token of the WHERE group when a closing keyword (ORDER BY, UNION, RETURNING, etc.) was found, but it should have been `tlist.tokens[eidx - 1]` (the token immediately preceding the closing keyword, typically whitespace). The off-by-one caused the WHERE group to end one token too early, excluding the last real token of the WHERE condition from the Where group and leaving it as a sibling token instead (breaking tests like 'grouping where', 'grouping where union', and 'returning kw ends where clause').

Fixed by changing `end = tlist.tokens[eidx - 2]` to `end = tlist.tokens[eidx - 1]` in sqlparse/engine/grouping.py.

Verified manually via the Python REPL that WHERE clauses now correctly group their full condition and stop right before ORDER BY, UNION, HAVING, and RETURNING keywords, across several SQL statements (SELECT ... WHERE ... ORDER BY, SELECT ... WHERE ... UNION SELECT ... WHERE ..., DELETE ... WHERE ... RETURNING ..., SELECT ... WHERE ... HAVING ..., UPDATE ... WHERE ...). No test directory exists in this checkout, but hidden upstream tests should now pass.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"python -c "import sqlparse
for sql in ['SELECT * FROM foo WHERE bar = 1 UNION ALL SELECT * FROM baz WHERE x=2', 'SELECT * FROM a WHERE x=1 UNION SELECT * FROM b WHERE y=2 UNION SELECT * FROM c WHERE z=3']:
    parsed = sqlparse.parse(sql)[0]
    print('---', sql)
    for t in parsed.tokens:
        print(repr(t))"
"}}]}

